// Typed channels: a bounded ring buffer or an unbuffered rendezvous between
// green threads, plus `select` for waiting on several at once.
//
// Stage-2 port of `runtime/chan/` (#1365). Both exist during the port;
//
// Semantics are the spec's (SPEC §16.2-16.3, mirroring
// Go): send on a closed channel is an error, receive on a closed-and-drained
// channel yields `(zero, false)`, a nil channel blocks forever, `close` wakes
// every blocked peer, and `select` picks uniformly at random among the cases
// ready at the moment it runs. Buffer discipline, wait-queue order and the
// select winner-take-all rule are unchanged — a channel that is "better" but
// different would deadlock programs that work today.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// THERE IS ONLY ONE CHANNEL TYPE HERE, AND THAT IS THE ABI'S CHOICE
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// `runtime/chan/` is generic (`Chan(T)`) and therefore needs `ChanVTable` — nine
// function pointers per instantiation — so that `select` can drive cases whose
// `T` differ. ABI.md §11 then collapses all of it: "every `chan<T>` a Bit
// program declares is realized at the runtime boundary as a channel of one
// 8-byte word (`WordChan = Chan(u64)`) — never a per-`T` instantiation".
//
// So the vtable's entire job is to bridge a genericity the ABI does not have.
// This port is `WordChan` and calls its own operations directly. That removes
// the indirection rather than reimplementing it, which matters twice over:
// `@nosplit` REJECTS A CALL THROUGH A FUNCTION VALUE (E0075), so a vtable is
// not merely unnecessary here but unexpressible — and `SelectNode`'s reason for
// existing (a layout-compatible stand-in for a `Waiter` whose `T` select cannot
// name) goes with it, along with the `comptime` assert that policed it. One
// waiter shape, no cast, nothing to keep in sync.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// WHY EVERY FUNCTION HERE IS `@nosplit`, AND WHAT THAT COSTS
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// Task bodies must be `@nosplit` today (see `schedWorkerRun`'s comment: a
// safepoint snapshots registers into one process-wide frame and collects
// unsynchronized, so no worker loop may reach one — #1429). A `@nosplit` body
// may only call `@nosplit` functions, and channel operations are called FROM
// task bodies, so this module inherits the attribute wholesale.
//
// Three consequences, none of them chosen:
//
//   1. NO PANIC HERE. `panic` is still not reachable from a `@nosplit` body, so
//      `chanSend`/`chanClose` still can't raise "send on closed channel" or
//      "close of closed channel" themselves — both still report a `false`
//      return. The ABI wrapper now reads that boolean and raises the panic
//      itself: `chanwrap.bit`'s `rtChanSend`/`rtChanClose` (#2017, #2506).
//      Same trade `schedUnpark` already made, for the same reason.
//   2. NO CALLBACKS. `scanRegistryRoots(mark)` and `forEachBuffered(visit)`
//      take function pointers. Both are re-exposed as cursors the collector
//      drives (`chanRegHead`/`chanRegNext`, `chanBufferedAt`) — exactly what
//      `runtime/sched` did to `forEachOtherStack` for the same reason.
//   3. NO CLOCK. `selectRandom` seeds from `monoNs()`; a clock is a syscall or
//      an extern call. See `selectSeed` for what replaces it.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// THE PARK RACE, WHICH IS THE ONE REAL DESIGN CHANGE IN THIS FILE
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// `runtime/chan/` links a waiter onto a channel's queue from inside `park`'s `setup`
// callback — "never before, and never after". That timing is the whole
// correctness argument: `setup` runs strictly after the task's context is saved
// and its state is visibly `.parked`, so an unparker can never see a waiter
// belonging to a task that is not yet parkable.
//
// THAT HOOK DOES NOT EXIST HERE. `schedPark` has no `ParkFn`, because passing
// one is a call through a function value, which `@nosplit` rejects (E0075).
// `runtime/sched`'s own comment names this as the seam "#1365 will need closed
// properly", so it is the inherited problem rather than a new one.
//
// This port closes it from the other end. A waiter is registered BEFORE parking,
// under the channel lock — which makes the waiter visible early rather than
// late, so the lost-wakeup risk moves from "a waker cannot find the task" to "a
// waker finds the task before it has finished parking". The second is
// detectable and the first is not: `schedUnpark` compare-and-exchanges
// `taskParked -> taskRunnable` and RETURNS FALSE when the task was not parked,
// so a waker can see the case exactly. `chanWake` therefore waits for the
// parking task to arrive before unparking it.
//
// WHY THAT WAIT TERMINATES, which is the only thing that makes this sound:
// a task appears on a wait queue only while it is RUNNING (it pushed itself
// there), and green threads here are not preemptible, so it holds its worker's
// CPU continuously from the push to the context switch — a lock release and a
// call. `taskParked` is stored by that worker immediately after the switch
// saves the context. So the waited-for transition is already in flight on
// another OS thread, with no lock in between that the waker could be holding.
//
// This is the SAME liveness assumption `runtime/spinlock` already makes of
// every critical section in the runtime — that a thread holding a word another
// thread spins on continues to be scheduled — not a new class of hazard. It is
// bounded anyway (`WAKE_MAX_SPINS`) so that a wedged scheduler degrades into a
// dropped wakeup rather than a hung process, and it spins on the same
// architectural hint instructions `runtime/spinlock` and the scheduler's idle
// backoff use.
//
// A WELCOME CONSEQUENCE: registering early removes the need for `setup`'s
// recheck. `runtime/chan/` must re-test the channel condition inside the callback
// because the gap between its own failed poll and registration is exactly when
// another thread could have made the case ready. Registering under the same
// lock acquisition that observed "not ready" closes that gap by construction,
// so there is no window to recheck. The behaviour is identical; one of the two
// mechanisms that produced it is simply no longer needed.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// MEMORY: CALLER-OWNED BLOCKS, AND A PER-TASK SCRATCH AREA
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// A channel, a ring buffer and a select descriptor array are word-indexed
// blocks the caller supplies, exactly as `runtime/sched`, `runtime/alloc` and
// `runtime/gc` take theirs. Nothing here allocates.
//
// A waiter is different: `runtime/chan/` puts it on the blocking function's own
// stack frame (`var w: Waiter`), which this port cannot do — `ptrOf` on a local
// is rejected inside a `@nosplit` body (#1421), so a local's address cannot be
// handed to a queue. And the ABI's entry points (`bit_rt_chan_send(ch, value)`)
// have no spare parameter for scratch, so the caller cannot supply it either.
//
// So a waiter lives at the base of the parking task's OWN STACK, in the words
// immediately above the self-pointer and tag `schedTaskInit` writes there. That
// is the same "the running task's stack is the one piece of per-thread state
// that needs no help from the operating system" trick `schedCurrentTask` rests
// on, reused rather than reinvented. It has the lifetime a waiter needs exactly
// — the scratch dies with the stack it belongs to, which is precisely when the
// task that could be waiting on it is gone — and it costs no allocation.
//
// The area is the LOWEST `chanScratchWords` words of the stack, and a stack
// grows down from its top, so the scratch is the last memory a deepening call
// chain would reach. A task that overran it has already overflowed its stack.
//
// 267 words = 2136 bytes out of a 64KiB stack: the eight fixed words below (word
// 7 is the SEAM-4 error slot), `chanMaxSelect` waiter nodes, two
// `chanMaxSelect`-entry index arrays, the SEAM-4 recv-ok flag past them, and
// #2994's fs-read-all-error flag and #3280's iface-as-ok flag past that
// (both chan-unrelated; documented here only because this is where the total
// width is). The layout itself
// (`chanScratchWords`, `scratchOf`, the `scr*` word offsets) is a property of
// the task block and lives in `runtime/sched/scratch.bit`, imported below —
// see that file for why it moved out of the chan<->root cycle (#1612).

import { spinAcquire, spinRelease, spinCellAt } from "../"
import { taskAt, taskState, taskParked, schedUnpark, schedPark } from "../sched"
import { schedCurrentTask } from "../sched"
import { scratchOf, schedCurrentScratch, scrCounter, scrSelDone, scrSelWinner, scrElem, scrOk } from "../sched"
import { scrNodes, scrOrder, scrShuffled, scrRecvOk } from "../sched"

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Channel block
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// `runtime/chan/`'s `Chan(T)` fields, in declaration order, as a word-indexed block:
//
//   0   lock        an i32 cell, addressed via `spinCellAt`
//   1   closed
//   2   buf         base address of the caller-owned ring storage (0 when cap 0)
//   3   cap
//   4   head
//   5   count
//   6   sendq head        the two wait queues, each a singly-linked FIFO of
//   7   sendq tail        waiter addresses under this channel's lock
//   8   recvq head
//   9   recvq tail
//   10  isRef       whether a buffered word is itself a GC reference (ABI §11)
//   11  regNext     intrusive link for the root-scanning registry
//   12  sched       the scheduler this channel's wakeups go through
//
// `sched` is a field here where `runtime/chan/` threads `sp: *Scheduler` through
// every call. Forced by the ABI: `bit_rt_chan_send(ch, value)` has no scheduler
// parameter and its arity is fixed, so a channel must be able to reach one on
// its own. Equivalent — there is one process-wide scheduler (ABI.md §9) — and
// it also makes `chanClose` callable from a non-worker thread, which the
// signature already allowed and this file's tests rely on.
export const chanWords: int = 13

const chLock: int = 0
const chClosed: int = 1
const chBuf: int = 2
const chCap: int = 3
const chHead: int = 4
const chCount: int = 5
const chSendHead: int = 6
const chSendTail: int = 7
const chRecvHead: int = 8
const chRecvTail: int = 9
const chIsRef: int = 10
const chRegNext: int = 11
const chSched: int = 12

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Waiter block
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// `runtime/chan/`'s `Waiter`, field for field and in the same order. It kept that
// order to stay layout-compatible with `SelectNode`; here there is only one
// shape, so the order is preserved simply because there is no reason to disturb
// a working structure.
//
//   0  task      the parked task's address
//   1  elem      address of the value slot (in for a send, out for a receive)
//   2  ok        address of the outcome slot
//   3  select    address of the select-shared block, 0 for a plain waiter
//   4  caseIdx   which select case this registration is
//   5  next      queue link
const wTask: int = 0
const wElem: int = 1
const wOk: int = 2
const wSelect: int = 3
const wCase: int = 4
const wNext: int = 5
const waiterWords: int = 6

// The per-task scratch word offsets (`scrCounter` .. `scrRecvOk`) live in
// `runtime/sched/scratch.bit` and are imported above: the scratch area is a
// property of the task block, not of channels (#1612). Word 8..199 are the
// `chanMaxSelect` waiter nodes and word 200..263 the two select index arrays,
// both addressed relative to `scrNodes`/`scrOrder`/`scrShuffled` by the code in
// this module.

// `runtime/chan/`'s `max_select_cases`, unchanged: select's registrations are
// fixed-size and preallocated (Power of 10 — no unbounded resource after
// startup). Nobody writes a 32-way select; the constant is a bound, not a
// budget.
export const chanMaxSelect: int = 32

// Select case direction, matching ABI.md §11's `SelectCaseDesc.dir` encoding
// (0 recv / 1 send) — codegen writes these, so they are ABI and not free.
export const chanDirRecv: int = 0
export const chanDirSend: int = 1

// `SelectCaseDesc { dir, chan, word, ok }`, ABI.md §11's 32-byte descriptor,
// as word indices. `word` is IN/OUT: a send case's value on the way in, a recv
// case's received value on the way out.
export const selCaseWords: int = 4
const scDir: int = 0
const scChan: int = 1
const scWord: int = 2
const scOk: int = 3

// Waiter node `i` of a task's scratch area.
@nosplit fn nodeAt(scr: *i64, i: int): *i64 {
  return scr + scrNodes + i * waiterWords
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Wait queues
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// `runtime/chan/`'s `WaitQueue(W)`: a singly-linked FIFO of waiter addresses guarded
// by the owning channel's lock. Two of them per channel, so each operation
// takes the head/tail slot pair to work on rather than a queue object — the
// same "a structure is a block plus an offset" shape the rest of the port uses.
//
// FIFO is the discipline, not an accident: it is what makes a channel fair
// between senders blocked on a full buffer.

@nosplit fn wqPush(ch: *i64, headSlot: int, w: *i64) {
  *(w + wNext) = 0
  let tail = *(ch + headSlot + 1)
  if (tail == 0) {
    *(ch + headSlot) = int(w)
  } else {
    *(taskAt(tail) + wNext) = int(w)
  }
  *(ch + headSlot + 1) = int(w)
}

@nosplit fn wqPop(ch: *i64, headSlot: int): int {
  let head = *(ch + headSlot)
  if (head == 0) {
    return 0
  }
  let next = *(taskAt(head) + wNext)
  *(ch + headSlot) = next
  if (next == 0) {
    *(ch + headSlot + 1) = 0
  }
  *(taskAt(head) + wNext) = 0
  return head
}

// Unlink a specific waiter. Only ever used to cancel a losing select
// registration, and a no-op when the target is absent — either a completer
// already popped it, or it was never registered because the winner resolved
// first. `runtime/chan/`'s `WaitQueue.remove`, including that tolerance.
//
// The walk is bounded by the queue's own length, which is bounded by the number
// of live tasks; `chanMaxWaiters` is the runaway backstop, the same shape
// `schedRegCount` uses on the task registry.
const chanMaxWaiters: int = 16777216

@nosplit fn wqRemove(ch: *i64, headSlot: int, target: int) {
  let prev = 0
  let cur = *(ch + headSlot)
  let n = 0
  while (cur != 0 && n < chanMaxWaiters) {
    let next = *(taskAt(cur) + wNext)
    if (cur == target) {
      if (prev == 0) {
        *(ch + headSlot) = next
      } else {
        *(taskAt(prev) + wNext) = next
      }
      if (*(ch + headSlot + 1) == cur) {
        *(ch + headSlot + 1) = prev
      }
      *(taskAt(cur) + wNext) = 0
      return
    }
    prev = cur
    cur = next
    n = n + 1
  }
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Waking a parked peer
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// See the header for why this waits and why the wait terminates. The bound
// exists so that termination is provable by READING the loop rather than by
// trusting the scheduler, exactly as `schedWorkerRun`'s trip bound is.
//
// Exhausting it drops a wakeup, which is a hang for the peer — but the only way
// to reach it is a scheduler that has already stopped running the parking task,
// in which case the peer was never going to be resumed anyway. Spinning forever
// would convert that into a hung waker as well.
const WAKE_MAX_SPINS: int = 1000000000

@nosplit fn chanWake(s: *i64, task: int) {
  let t = taskAt(task)
  let n = 0
  while (n < WAKE_MAX_SPINS) {
    if (atomicLoad(t + taskState) == taskParked) {
      schedUnpark(s, task)
      return
    }
    asm {
      arm64 { 0xD503203F } // yield
      x64 { 0xF3, 0x90 }   // pause
    }
    n = n + 1
  }
}

// True if this waiter may be completed by the caller.
//
// `runtime/chan/`'s `claim`, unchanged in mechanism. A plain waiter always succeeds:
// it sits on exactly one queue and nothing else contends for it. A select
// registration claims by CAS on its shared `done` word, so exactly one of the
// several channels a select is parked on can complete it; every other channel's
// attempt loses the CAS and must treat the entry as if it had never been queued.
// This is Go's `sudog.isSelect`/`selectDone` design, which is where `runtime/chan/`
// took it from.
@nosplit fn claim(w: *i64): bool {
  let sel = *(w + wSelect)
  if (sel == 0) {
    return true
  }
  return atomicCmpxchg(taskAt(sel), 0, 1)
}

// Record the winning case (select only) and wake the waiter's task.
@nosplit fn finish(s: *i64, w: *i64) {
  let sel = *(w + wSelect)
  if (sel != 0) {
    *(taskAt(sel) + 1) = *(w + wCase)
  }
  chanWake(s, *(w + wTask))
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Ring buffer — cap > 0 only
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// WRAPPING IS A CONDITIONAL SUBTRACT, NOT A `%` (#3318), and it is spelled
// branchlessly on purpose — both alternatives were assembled and counted.
//
// `%` on a signed `int` by a divisor the backend has to load lowers to `sdiv` +
// `msub` plus a divide-by-zero trap branch. Writing the wrap as an `if` removed
// that and cost MORE: a two-armed `if` joins with block parameters, and every
// value live across the join is copied into place on BOTH arms, so `popBuf`
// went 31 -> 37 instructions with seven `mov`s of shuffle in the middle (#1852).
//
// `wrapIndex` has one arm, so there is no join and nothing to shuffle: a `sub`,
// an arithmetic shift, an and-not and a `sub`. The precondition is what makes it
// exact, and it holds here and only here — both indices are already less than
// TWICE the capacity, `head` because it is an index (`head < cap`) and
// `head + count` because `count <= cap`. Those two invariants are established by
// `chanInit` and preserved by exactly these two bodies, so the bound is
// checkable by reading this file.

// `idx` reduced modulo `capacity`, for `0 <= idx < 2 * capacity` ONLY. Outside
// that range it is not a modulo and does not claim to be.
//
// `(idx - capacity) >> 63` is the sign of the difference broadcast over the
// word: all ones when `idx < capacity`, zero otherwise. `~` flips it, so the
// `&` yields either nothing to subtract or the whole capacity.
@nosplit fn wrapIndex(idx: int, capacity: int): int {
  return idx - (capacity & ~((idx - capacity) >> 63))
}

@nosplit fn pushBuf(ch: *i64, value: int) {
  let buf = taskAt(*(ch + chBuf))
  let count = *(ch + chCount)
  *(buf + wrapIndex(*(ch + chHead) + count, *(ch + chCap))) = value
  *(ch + chCount) = count + 1
}

@nosplit fn popBuf(ch: *i64): int {
  let buf = taskAt(*(ch + chBuf))
  let head = *(ch + chHead)
  let value = *(buf + head)
  *(ch + chHead) = wrapIndex(head + 1, *(ch + chCap))
  *(ch + chCount) = *(ch + chCount) - 1
  return value
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Direct handoffs
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// These skip the buffer entirely, and are also how an unbuffered channel
// (cap 0) always moves a value. `runtime/chan/`'s `tryHandoffToReceiver` /
// `tryHandoffFromSender` / `promoteQueuedSender`, unchanged: each pops until it
// finds a waiter it can claim, because an unclaimable one belongs to a select
// that another channel already won and must be discarded rather than retried.

@nosplit fn tryHandoffToReceiver(ch: *i64, value: int, s: *i64): bool {
  let n = 0
  while (n < chanMaxWaiters) {
    let addr = wqPop(ch, chRecvHead)
    if (addr == 0) {
      return false
    }
    let w = taskAt(addr)
    if (claim(w)) {
      *taskAt(*(w + wElem)) = value
      *taskAt(*(w + wOk)) = 1
      finish(s, w)
      return true
    }
    n = n + 1
  }
  return false
}

@nosplit fn tryHandoffFromSender(ch: *i64, valueOut: *i64, s: *i64): bool {
  let n = 0
  while (n < chanMaxWaiters) {
    let addr = wqPop(ch, chSendHead)
    if (addr == 0) {
      return false
    }
    let w = taskAt(addr)
    if (claim(w)) {
      *valueOut = *taskAt(*(w + wElem))
      *taskAt(*(w + wOk)) = 1
      finish(s, w)
      return true
    }
    n = n + 1
  }
  return false
}

// After a value leaves the buffer, pull one blocked sender into the freed slot.
// Only possible when the buffer had been full, and it is what preserves FIFO
// order across buffered sends — without it a sender that blocked on a full
// buffer would be overtaken by every later sender that found room.
@nosplit fn promoteQueuedSender(ch: *i64, s: *i64) {
  let n = 0
  while (n < chanMaxWaiters) {
    let addr = wqPop(ch, chSendHead)
    if (addr == 0) {
      return
    }
    let w = taskAt(addr)
    if (claim(w)) {
      pushBuf(ch, *taskAt(*(w + wElem)))
      *taskAt(*(w + wOk)) = 1
      finish(s, w)
      return
    }
    n = n + 1
  }
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Non-blocking attempts — the channel lock must be held
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// `runtime/chan/`'s `attemptSend`/`attemptRecv`, in the same order, because the order
// IS the semantics: a waiting receiver takes precedence over the buffer (so an
// unbuffered channel works at all, and so a rendezvous does not pointlessly
// round-trip through storage), and on the receive side a buffered value takes
// precedence over a waiting sender (so FIFO across the buffer holds).

@nosplit fn attemptSend(ch: *i64, value: int, s: *i64): bool {
  if (tryHandoffToReceiver(ch, value, s)) {
    return true
  }
  if (*(ch + chCount) < *(ch + chCap)) {
    pushBuf(ch, value)
    return true
  }
  return false
}

// `attemptRecv` reports through a status rather than an out-parameter `ok`.
//
// `runtime/chan/` writes `ok` through a pointer because its caller always has a
// stack local to point at. This port's caller does not: a local's address is
// unavailable inside a `@nosplit` body (#1421), so an out-parameter `ok` would
// force every receive — including the non-blocking fast path — to reach for the
// per-task scratch, and that would make `chanRecv` fault when called from a
// thread that is not a task. A status return keeps the fast path task-free,
// which is what lets a non-task thread drain a channel.
const rcvNone: int = 0
const rcvValue: int = 1
const rcvClosed: int = 2

@nosplit fn attemptRecv(ch: *i64, valueOut: *i64, s: *i64): int {
  if (*(ch + chCount) > 0) {
    *valueOut = popBuf(ch)
    promoteQueuedSender(ch, s)
    return rcvValue
  }
  if (tryHandoffFromSender(ch, valueOut, s)) {
    return rcvValue
  }
  if (*(ch + chClosed) != 0) {
    // The zero word, and `ok` false to say so. For a reference element that
    // zero IS a null pointer, which is the whole reason the two-result receive
    // exists (ABI.md §11) — a receiver must be able to tell "closed" from "a
    // real value" before dereferencing it.
    *valueOut = 0
    return rcvClosed
  }
  return rcvNone
}
